You are auditing a post-publish fixup pass of the Harn release flow. The
release artifact for `{{ target_version }}` has already shipped:
`{{ fixup_tag }}` was pushed during the original `publish-release.yml`
run, and crates.io + the GitHub release are live for that exact commit.
The open Release PR (`{{ fixup_pr_url }}`) is paperwork — its job is to
land the Cargo.toml/CHANGELOG bump on `{{ base_branch }}`, not to
influence what was published.

What the harness already did, deterministically:

- Force-pushed the release branch onto a fresh `{{ base_branch }}`
  snapshot.
- Injected the originally-shipped `## v{{ target_version }}` body
  (verbatim from `git show {{ fixup_tag }}:CHANGELOG.md`) above the
  next-older version heading.
- **Preserved `## Unreleased` exactly as it appears on
  `{{ base_branch }}`.** Entries that landed in `## Unreleased` after
  `{{ fixup_tag }}` was pushed describe behavior that is NOT in the
  shipped binary, so they belong in the NEXT release. The harness
  did not move them into v{{ target_version }}.

Constraints — non-negotiable:

- The `{{ fixup_tag }}` tag stays where it is. Do not recommend
  retagging, force-pushing, or moving it. Re-shipping a published
  version is impossible (crates.io is immutable).
- Do not author a new release section. The shipped v{{ target_version }}
  body is authoritative.
- Do not recommend folding `## Unreleased` entries into
  v{{ target_version }} — that would mislabel the changelog (those
  entries are not in the v{{ target_version }} binary).

Your job: a tight verification pass. Use only local read-only tools.
Inspect the merged CHANGELOG.md and at least one PR/diff/file source
that the post-tag drift touched. Then return a short Audit block.

Confirm:

- The injected v{{ target_version }} body matches the originally-shipped
  release notes (no edits crept in).
- `## Unreleased` is intact and contains entries that genuinely should
  ship in a future release (not in v{{ target_version }}).

Flag, if any:

- Unreleased entries that look like they belong INSIDE
  v{{ target_version }} (e.g., a fix for behavior that was already
  shipped) — those would need a manual move from Unreleased to
  v{{ target_version }} before merge.
- Anything in v{{ target_version }} that doesn't match the shipped tag.

Do not include `BEGIN_DRAFT_CHANGELOG` markers.
